I would be very surprised if it did.

Finnish is a Finougrian language, like Estonian, the Samen language, and
Hungarian. These languages originate from the Mongolian hordes that
thrashed Europe in the fifth (?) century AD.

As a consequence, they're more related to languages from the other side of
the Ural mountains than to those of ancient Europe. In particular, they
have almost no Greek and Latin influences. Only the modern-day vocabulary
would probably have some or lots of Indo-European influences (don't we all
adopt English words?).  :-(

There are only a few more of those languages in Europe: Basque is the one
that springs to mind, and I'm not sure about Albanese (Albanian?).

